# VRAM preset for a 24GB CUDA card (e.g. RTX 3090/4090).
#
# CONFIDENCE: measured, on a rented RTX 4090 (24564MiB reported by
# nvidia-smi). Both CHUNK_SIZE and MAX_RES below were smoke-tested with
# PROCESS_LENGTH set to a single outer chunk and peak VRAM read off
# nvidia-smi mid-run:
#   CHUNK_SIZE=420, MAX_RES=768 -> 23044MiB (94% - stable, not climbing, but
#     too little slack for an ~83-chunk unattended run)
#   CHUNK_SIZE=210, MAX_RES=768 -> 22032MiB (90%, ~2.5GB headroom) <- chosen
# Note CHUNK_SIZE has a much smaller effect than you'd expect: halving it
# only recovered ~1GB. That's because at max_res=768, VRAM is dominated by
# the fixed per-window attention/model cost (DepthCrafter's internal window
# is WINDOW_SIZE=70 frames regardless of the outer CHUNK_SIZE), not by how
# many windows get chained into one chunk - don't bother pushing CHUNK_SIZE
# lower for more headroom, it's not where the VRAM is going. If you need
# more margin than this preset gives, drop MAX_RES instead (that's the
# actual VRAM-hungry knob), or enable CPU_OFFLOAD.
#
# MAX_RES=768 itself (vs. the scripts' own 384 default, sized for a 12GB
# ROCm card with no working flash-attention) is the one really worth having
# on a 24GB CUDA card: it's DepthCrafter's internal depth-estimation
# resolution, so it improves actual depth-map detail across the whole video,
# unlike CHUNK_SIZE which only reduces chunk-boundary crossfade seams.
#
# TILE_NUM: DO NOT just lower this for more VRAM headroom on AMD/ROCm
# hardware - see presets/16GB.env's TILE_NUM note for the full explanation.
# Measured on the 12GB dev card: tile_num=2 needs manual chunked-attention
# fallback (chunked_attention.py) to avoid OOM at all on ROCm (no working
# flash/efficient kernel here), and that fallback runs ~9-11x slower per
# chunk than tile_num=4, with real tail-latency spikes (one tile hit 285s
# for a single step). A bigger card doesn't fix this - it's a compute
# scaling problem (attention cost ~ tile_area^2 without a real efficient
# kernel), not a VRAM one.
#
# On NVIDIA/CUDA (this preset's actual target): flash-attention works, so
# chunked_attention.py's slow fallback never triggers (it always tries the
# real kernel first) and lowering TILE_NUM is a genuine quality/VRAM-usage
# win (fewer/bigger tiles = fewer seam-blend boundaries).
#
# MEASURED on this rented RTX 4090 (tuned directly against a pre-built splat
# store via inpainting_inference.py --max_iters, enable_model_cpu_offload=True,
# num_inference_steps=8):
#   TILE_NUM=4 (old default) -> 19GB one-time spike (initial load/first
#     pass), then settles at a steady 6-7GB - massively overprovisioned,
#     ~17.5GB sitting idle the whole run on this card.
#   TILE_NUM=2 -> settles at a steady 21GB (the ramp to that plateau looks
#     like a "19GB peak" if you only watch briefly, but it keeps climbing -
#     21GB is the real number). ~3.5GB headroom. <- chosen
#   TILE_NUM=1 -> OOMs. Expected: going 2->1 roughly triples/quadruples
#     pixels-per-tile (one tile instead of four), which pushes well past
#     21GB even with real flash-attention's linear (not quadratic) scaling.
#     Confirmed empirically, not just predicted from the math.
#
# NUM_INFERENCE_STEPS is a separate, VRAM-free quality lever (same memory
# per step, just more of them) - worth raising above 8 independently of any
# of the above, still unverified how far. Pinned at the validated baseline
# (8) below rather than left to the script's own default, same reasoning as
# CPU_OFFLOAD above - bump it yourself once you've eyeballed the quality/
# time tradeoff on a real chunk.
#
# DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE/DECODE_LATENTS_CHUNK_SIZE: MEASURED, not a guess anymore -
# only control the VAE-decode step (cheap relative to the tiled UNet
# attention above), moving them made ~1GB difference in a 24GB budget. Not
# worth chasing further; left at 4/4.
#
# FRAMES_CHUNK: raised from the script's own default (5) once the math on a
# real 34815-frame episode made stage 2's total runtime a hard budget
# problem (~6.3 days at frames_chunk=5, on a rental with a ~2-day/$25
# budget). Each iteration pays a fixed per-chunk cost (VAE encode, tile
# bookkeeping, re-processing the OVERLAP=3 frame overlap) on top of the
# per-frame UNet/attention cost - a bigger frames_chunk amortizes that fixed
# cost over more frames. MEASURED on this rented RTX 4090 (TILE_NUM=2,
# num_inference_steps=8, avg/last_iter time off inpainting_inference.py's
# own output, divided by frames_chunk for an effective s/frame):
#   frames_chunk=5  (script default) -> 15.62 s/frame (78.1s/iter, avg of 4)
#   frames_chunk=23 (StereoCrafter's own upstream default) -> 7.91 s/frame
#     (181.9s/iter avg of 5), peak VRAM ~21-22GB, stable across iterations
#   frames_chunk=30 -> 7.53 s/frame (226.0s/iter, 2nd-iteration reading -
#     the first iteration is always slower, model-load/allocator warmup, so
#     don't trust a single first reading), peak VRAM ~22.6GB <- chosen
#   frames_chunk=40 -> 7.25 s/frame (290.0s/iter, single sample) but peaked
#     23.7GB - only ~0.8GB headroom left on this card, too thin for an
#     unattended multi-day run (VRAM has +-1-1.5GB run-to-run noise
#     elsewhere in this file's own measurements)
# Gains diminish fast: 5->23 roughly halved s/frame, but 23->40 (a much
# bigger jump) only bought another ~8%, right at the OOM edge. Not worth
# testing further - 30 is the practical ceiling on this card, giving most of
# the available speedup with real headroom left.
#
# IMPORTANT: frames_chunk is part of the checkpoint's run_params match
# (inpainting_inference.py ~line 515) - changing it mid-run (e.g. lowering
# it overnight, raising it while attended) invalidates the ENTIRE checkpoint
# and restarts stage 2 from scratch, not just the in-flight chunk. Pick one
# value for the whole run.
#
# ---- STAGE 2 SPEED PASS (see inpainting_inference.py's docstring for the
# full writeup of each flag) ----
# The 7.53 s/frame number below predates the CFG/cpu-offload/work_scale
# changes - at 34815 frames that's ~73h against a ~2-day/$25 budget, i.e.
# doesn't fit. Killing CFG (min/max_guidance_scale=1.0, ~2x - the single
# biggest item) and disabling cpu_offload (~15%, CFG-off roughly halves the
# VRAM it was trading away) are both safe defaults now, set below.
# WORK_SCALE=0.5 is the big remaining lever (runs the diffusion model at
# half resolution, upsamples back before compositing against the FULL-
# resolution warp/mask) - MEASURED on the local 12GB ROCm card (not this
# card - no CUDA hardware available to re-measure there) at ~4.1x over
# WORK_SCALE=1.0 with the other fixes already applied, visually verified
# (diff against WORK_SCALE=1.0: mean abs pixel diff 0.097/255, differences
# confined to thin high-contrast edges - see presets/12GB.env's comment for
# the full readout). The *tiled pixel area* math behind that speedup
# (~4.5x fewer pixels/step) is hardware-independent, so a similar win is
# expected here, but CONFIRM WITH --bench_iters (and --dump_frames to
# sanity-check output) against a real chunk of this episode before trusting
# it for the unattended multi-hour run - this exact number (7.53 s/frame)
# is what happens if you skip that step and something doesn't transfer.
#
# Usage:
#   source presets/24GB.env && ./run_stereo.sh input.mp4

# ---- stage 1 (depth splatting) ----
export CHUNK_SIZE=210       # measured peak 22032MiB/24564MiB with CPU_OFFLOAD=None,
                             # MAX_RES=768 - see header for the 420 vs 210 comparison
export MAX_RES=768          # measured, see header - the real quality win on this card
export WINDOW_SIZE=70       # unchanged - validated for the cross-chunk consistency fix
export WINDOW_OVERLAP=25
export CPU_OFFLOAD=None
export STAGE1_DECODE_CHUNK_SIZE=16  # MEASURED this session on this card (see
                                     # run_stereo.sh's own comment for the
                                     # full readout) - ~6.5% off total depth-
                                     # pass wall time. 24/32 both OOM'd here.

# ---- stage 2 (inpainting) ----
export TILE_NUM=1           # MEASURED this session on this exact card (real rental
                             # RTX 4090): at WORK_SCALE=0.5, TILE_NUM=1 (no tiling)
                             # peaks ~20.6GB/24.5GB (real headroom) and is ~13%
                             # faster than TILE_NUM=2 (28.5s/iter vs 32.8s/iter,
                             # clean A/B on the same splat store/seed) - fewer,
                             # bigger forward passes beats more, smaller ones here.
                             # TILE_NUM=2 still OOMs at WORK_SCALE=1.0 if you ever
                             # need to go back to full resolution.
export FRAMES_CHUNK=45      # MEASURED this session on this card at TILE_NUM=1,
                             # WORK_SCALE=0.5: 45 -> 1.002 s/frame (~5.1% faster
                             # than 30's 1.056 s/frame), ~22.2GB/24.5GB peak
                             # (~2.3GB headroom). 60 measured slightly faster
                             # still (0.967 s/frame) but with thinner headroom
                             # (~2-3GB) for an unattended multi-hour run - 45
                             # chosen as the safer point; see run_stereo.sh
                             # comment header below for the original (stale,
                             # pre-TILE_NUM=1) 7.53 s/frame figure this replaces.
export DECODE_LATENTS_CHUNK_SIZE=4
export NUM_INFERENCE_STEPS=8  # validated baseline - VRAM-free to raise, see header
export ENABLE_MODEL_CPU_OFFLOAD=False  # CFG-off below roughly halves UNet activation
                                        # memory vs. what this preset originally measured
                                        # with cpu_offload=True - if this OOMs, re-enable
                                        # it before giving up WORK_SCALE/TILE_NUM headroom.
export CHUNKED_ATTENTION=False  # this card has a working flash/efficient SDPA kernel
                                 # (NVIDIA/CUDA) - chunked_attention.py's fallback never
                                 # triggers here, so this only costs a context-manager
                                 # entry per attention call for nothing. Verified safe to
                                 # disable on real CUDA hardware per chunked_attention.py's
                                 # own module docstring; UNVERIFIED specifically on THIS
                                 # card (no CUDA hardware available to confirm this session).
export MIN_GUIDANCE_SCALE=1.0  # kills CFG - ~2x, see inpainting_inference.py's docstring
export MAX_GUIDANCE_SCALE=1.0
export WORK_SCALE=0.5  # MEASURED ~4.1x on the 12GB ROCm card - see header. CONFIRM with
                        # --bench_iters against this episode before trusting it here.

# ---- resumability (leave this on - it's the safety net for the above being wrong) ----
export RESUME=True
export MAX_RETRIES=3
